 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Image Size Constraints:** Texturing hardware typically requires image dimensions to be powers of two. This causes jumps in rendering time at edge lengths of 512 and 1024 pixels [S2, unofficial].
-- **Matrix Inversion:** Unlike software warp implementations which use the inverse of the warp matrix to sample pixel values from the intermediate image, the texture-based method does not invert the matrix [S2, unofficial].
+- **Image Size Constraints:** Texturing hardware requires image dimensions to be powers of two, causing jumps in rendering time at edge lengths of 512 and 1024 pixels [S2, unofficial].
+- **Matrix Inversion:** Unlike software warps that invert the warp matrix to sample pixel values from the intermediate image, this method applies the non-inverted $W_{per}$ directly [S2, unofficial].
+- **Performance Overhead:** Higher overhead for small images compared to large ones [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 - **Time Complexity:** The warp time is negligible for large images, accounting for less than 2% of total rendering time on an SGI Onyx2 system [S2, unofficial].
 - **Memory Complexity:** Only the intermediate image needs to be transferred to graphics hardware, rather than the final (often larger) image [S2, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Faster than software warp for large images. However, it has higher overhead for small images [S2, unofficial].
-- **Scaling:** Rendering speed is practically unaffected by final image size due to hardware acceleration [S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** High speed for large images; bilinear interpolation is performed at no extra cost; preserves the software-based nature of the overall algorithm (easy parallelization, memory limits based on main memory rather than GPU memory) [S2, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Power-of-two texture size constraints can cause performance spikes; higher overhead for small images; perspective warp is significantly more expensive than parallel warp (approx. 45% slower on the tested PC hardware) because the perspective matrix is non-affine, requiring more floating-point operations (14 vs 8) if computed in software, though hardware mitigates this [S2, unofficial].
+- **Measured Performance:** Faster than software warp for large images; higher overhead for small images [S2, unofficial].
+- **Scaling:** Final image size practically does not affect rendering speed due to hardware acceleration [S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** High speed for large images; bilinear interpolation performed at no extra cost; preserves the software-based nature of the overall algorithm (easy parallelization, volume size limited by main memory rather than GPU memory) [S2, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Power-of-two texture constraints cause performance spikes; perspective warp is approximately 45% slower than parallel projection on tested PC hardware because the non-affine matrix requires 14 floating-point operations per pixel (6 multiplications, 6 additions, 2 divisions) versus 8 for parallel projection [S2, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** Large-image rendering in virtual environments where hardware texturing is available and main-memory-limited volume sizing is required [S2, unofficial].
+- **When not to use:** Small-image rendering due to higher overhead, or environments where power-of-two texture constraints cannot be accommodated [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method was described in the context of the Perspective Shear-Warp Algorithm for virtual environments [S2, unofficial]. It leverages existing OpenGL texture mapping capabilities to accelerate the warp step, which is a critical component of the shear-warp factorization [S1, unofficial]. The approach allows the shear-warp algorithm to maintain its advantages (such as easy parallelization and main-memory-limited volume size) while utilizing graphics hardware for the final transformation [S2, unofficial].
+The method was described in the context of the Perspective Shear-Warp Algorithm for virtual environments [S2, unofficial]. It leverages fixed-function OpenGL texture mapping capabilities to accelerate the warp step of the shear-warp factorization [S1, unofficial]. The approach maintains the shear-warp algorithm's core advantages, such as easy parallelization and main-memory-limited volume sizing, while offloading the final transformation to graphics hardware [S2, unofficial].
 
